---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { PartitionPruningLab } from '../../../../components/english/PartitionPruningLab'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import partitionPruningStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-partition-pruning.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'Partition Pruning in SQL: Why Filters May Not Prune | sql.sb'
const description =
  'Learn how partition pruning narrows a SQL scan, why a filter may not reach partitions, and how to verify engine-specific plans without assuming a performance gain.'
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="sql"
  styleUrls={[partitionPruningStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'SQL', key: 'sql' },
      { label: 'Partition pruning' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">SQL · storage and scan behavior</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">Partition pruning in SQL: why a filter may not prune</h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      A date filter can be logically correct and still leave a query scanning more partitions than
      expected. The key question is whether the engine can map the predicate to the table's
      partition metadata—not merely whether a <code>WHERE</code> clause exists.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      Partition pruning skips partitions that cannot match a predicate on the partition key. Use a
      type-aligned range on that key, then inspect the engine's plan or scan metrics to confirm
      which partitions and bytes were read. Expressions, implicit casts, mismatched columns,
      unavailable metadata, and engine-specific optimizer rules can change the outcome. A filter
      alone proves neither pruning nor a runtime improvement.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="pruning-scope">
      <h2 id="pruning-scope">What partition pruning changes</h2>
      <p>
        Suppose a transaction table is partitioned by <code>txn_date</code>. A query for one month
        should not need every date partition if the engine can determine that the other partition
        values are outside the requested range. Pruning narrows the candidate partitions before
        reading their data; it does not rewrite the table or remove rows from storage.
      </p>
      <p>
        The shared scan-layout teaching fixture compares 1,095 historical partitions with a fixed
        30-day window. Its illustrative snapshots show 30 partitions and 82 TB for the narrowed
        range versus 1,095 partitions and 3.0 PB for the full-history scan. Those figures are
        fixture values, not production measurements or a benchmark.
      </p>
      <PartitionPruningLab client:load />
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="typed-range">
      <h2 id="typed-range">Start with a type-aligned half-open range</h2>
      <p>
        If <code>txn_date</code> is a date partition key, express the requested calendar window
        directly on that column. A half-open range includes the start and excludes the next period's
        start, avoiding ambiguous end-of-day timestamps:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`SELECT txn_date, COUNT(*) AS transaction_count
FROM transactions
WHERE txn_date >= DATE '2026-09-01'
  AND txn_date < DATE '2026-10-01'
GROUP BY txn_date
ORDER BY txn_date;`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The dates above describe September 2026. In the teaching fixture, a 30-day range maps to 30
        of 1,095 date partitions. The exact SQL literal syntax and partition plan vary by engine;
        adapt the types and boundaries to the column definition, timezone convention, and query
        dialect. The expected result rows depend on the table's data; partition counts describe the
        scan scope, not the number of grouped rows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="why-not-pruned">
      <h2 id="why-not-pruned">Why a partition filter may not prune</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The predicate targets another column.</strong> Filtering on ingestion time does
          not necessarily constrain partitions created from business date.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The key is wrapped or converted.</strong> A function or cast around the partition
          column can make bounds harder to infer. Some optimizers rewrite particular expressions;
          others do not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Types or boundaries do not line up.</strong> Implicit coercion, string-formatted
          dates, timestamp timezones, or an inclusive end boundary can alter comparison semantics or
          prevent a simple partition-range inference.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Metadata or statistics are missing or stale.</strong> Partition pruning needs
          enough partition metadata to exclude whole partitions. Depending on the engine, statistics
          may affect plan choices or finer-grained file skipping; these mechanisms are related but
          not interchangeable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The filter is indirect.</strong> A join, disjunction, parameter, or runtime value
          may require dynamic pruning or an optimizer feature that is unavailable or not selected
          for this plan.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="info">
        <strong>
          Do not assume every expression blocks pruning—or that every engine rewrites it
        </strong>
        <p>
          For example, a function on <code>txn_date</code> may prevent static range inference in one
          engine, while another engine may simplify that expression. Confirm behavior for the
          deployed engine and version instead of treating one SQL shape as a universal rule.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="verify-plan">
      <h2 id="verify-plan">Verify the scan in the query plan</h2>
      <p>
        Use the engine's <code>EXPLAIN</code> output and, when available, the executed query
        profile. Names differ, but look for evidence that corresponds to the table's partition key
        and scan:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Confirm the table's actual partition column, type, granularity, and boundary convention.
        </li>
        <li>Check the plan's partition filter or equivalent planned partition list.</li>
        <li>Compare partitions considered, partitions read, bytes scanned, and files opened.</li>
        <li>
          For runtime or join-derived filters, inspect the executed plan and actual scan metrics.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If partition counts fall but bytes remain high, inspect file sizes, row-group statistics,
        and data distribution inside the selected partitions. If the plan shows no pruning, first
        verify semantics and metadata, then compare a direct typed predicate with the original
        expression. Do not infer a fix from elapsed time alone: cache state, concurrency,
        scheduling, and other work can change runtime independently of scan scope.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="related-mechanisms">
      <h2 id="related-mechanisms">Partition pruning is not data skipping or data skew</h2>
      <p>
        Partition pruning excludes whole partitions using partition values and engine metadata. Some
        systems also use file or row-group statistics—such as minimum and maximum values—to skip
        data within a selected partition. That finer-grained data skipping is a separate mechanism.
        Neither technique guarantees a particular runtime.
      </p>
      <p>
        Data skew is different again: it concerns uneven work distribution, such as one task
        receiving much more input than its peers after data is redistributed. A query can prune
        partitions successfully and still have skew inside the remaining work. See the separate
        explanation of <a href={getEnglishRoute('dataSkew')}>SQL data skew and long-tail tasks</a>.
        JOIN fanout is about repeated keys multiplying result rows, not about which storage
        partitions are scanned; see{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('joinDuplicateRows')}>why a SQL JOIN duplicates rows</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="english-article__related" aria-label="Related English resources">
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('sql')}>More English SQL resources</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('dataSkew')}>SQL data skew</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('joinDuplicateRows')}>SQL JOIN duplicate rows</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
